From 3a202040988a5a049ed68c0a44b067ad7e6c1e3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:38:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/22] perf(redis): one shared asyncio client per worker
 instead of a new pool per operation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug (B-14): rate_limit, presence_service, code_interpreter_capacity_service,
auth_exchange, twofa_pending, saml_state and the readiness probe each
called redis.asyncio.from_url() per operation and aclose()d it right after:
a new connection pool, TCP connect and AUTH for every rate-limit check,
presence ping and one-time code. Under load that is a connect storm against
Redis and a latency tax on every request.

Change: app/core/redis_client.get_redis() returns a lazily built client
bound to the running event loop (so a fresh loop per test never reuses a
dead loop's connections), 2s socket timeouts, health checks every 30s. All
seven call sites use it and no longer close what they get; the lifespan
closes it once on shutdown. The knowledge worker keeps its dedicated client
(blocking XREADGROUP should not share a pool with request handlers).

Tests: tests/test_redis_client_singleton.py — one build per loop/URL, close
then rebuild, different loop -> new client; 100 rate-limit checks + 100
presence pings + 50 codes -> exactly one client and zero aclose() calls.
The presence test that asserted a close per call now asserts the opposite.

